{
  "_about": {
    "purpose": "Single-file DS index for AI-assisted UI generation. Read this first when building any multi-component surface. Go to components/<name>.json only when you need full token/state detail for a specific component.",
    "structure": "Two-file system: (1) ds-index.json — this file, fast overview of all components; (2) components/<name>.json — full detail per component (tokens, all states, markup, a11y). Read the index to pick components, then fetch individual files only as needed.",
    "philosophy": "AI documentation is not prose. It is structured constraints. Verbose guidelines belong on the human doc page. This file answers: what exists, what class to use, when to use it, and what to combine it with.",
    "example": {
      "wrong": "Use the primary button for the most important action. It has a filled background and stands out visually.",
      "right": {
        "class": "pos-button pos-button-primary",
        "role": "primary_action",
        "max_per_screen": 1
      }
    },
    "updated": "2026-10-02: catalog completed — every component spec and component CSS has an entry (checked by npm run check:catalog); removed the non-existent \"toggle\" entry (pos-toggle.css is docs-site only — the switch is toggle-btn); forms entry fixed to the real pos-form-* classes. An entry lists \"specs\" / \"css\" when its key differs from the spec / CSS file name."
  },
  "tokens": {
    "source": "modules/common-styling/public/assets/style/pos-config.css",
    "primitives": "modules/common-styling/public/assets/style/pos-primitives.css",
    "component_detail": "modules/common-styling/public/assets/tokens/components/<name>.json",
    "semantic_reference": "modules/common-styling/public/assets/tokens/token-map.json",
    "semantic_reference_note": "token-map.json is the authoritative AI-readable token catalog. Contains every semantic token with: css variable name, figma name, resolved light/dark values, chain, status, and description. Read this when you need to understand what a token means, when to use it, or when generating UI that must use the correct semantic token for a given role."
  },
